F.append(dict(
num='1.2', file='fig1-02_ar-nexafs',
where='Ch. 1, Sec. 1.5 (transition dipole moments and polarization). Place the paragraph directly after the existing paragraph that begins "Chemical composition is only one mode of the chemical state" and ends "the central idea developed in Chapter 2."',
cap='<b>Angle-resolved NEXAFS of an edge-on ZnPc film.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> A photon near 285 eV promotes an electron from a C 1s orbital localized on one carbon atom into a π* orbital delocalized over the conjugated ring, whose transition dipole μ lies along the ring normal. '
    '<b>b)</b> In the edge-on film the π* transition dipole μ_{π*} lies at angle α from the surface normal n̂, and the electric field E lies at angle θ from n̂. '
    '<b>c)</b> Mass absorption μ_{m} for θ = 20° (red) to 90° (black), with the π* (283.6 to 286.0 eV, red) and σ* (291 to 300 eV, gray) integration windows shaded. '
    '<b>d)</b> Integrated π* (red, filled) and σ* (black, open) intensity against cos^{2}θ with linear fits. The vector-orbital model gives α = 74.2 ± 0.5° for π* and the plane-orbital model gives γ = 67.9 ± 0.8° for σ*.',
par='Angle-resolved NEXAFS makes this coupling explicit (Fig. 1.2) [Stöhr 1992; Ade and Hitchcock 2008]. At the carbon K edge the initial state is a C 1s orbital confined to a single atom, while the final state of the lowest resonances is a π* orbital spread over the conjugated ring (Fig. 1.2a). The π* orbital is built from p orbitals normal to the ring, so its transition dipole μ_{π*} points along the ring normal, and by Fermi\'s golden rule the absorption scales as |E·μ|^{2}, the squared cosine of the angle between the field and the dipole. In an edge-on ZnPc film the rings stand close to upright on the substrate, which places μ_{π*} near the substrate plane at a tilt α from the surface normal n̂ (Fig. 1.2b). Rotating E from θ = 20° to 90° relative to n̂ therefore raises the π* resonances near 285 eV and lowers the σ* band between 291 and 300 eV, whose transition dipoles lie in the ring plane (Fig. 1.2c). Integrating each window and plotting the result against cos^{2}θ gives two straight lines of opposite slope (Fig. 1.2d). Fitting the π* intensity to the vector-orbital form I = A[1 + ½(3cos^{2}θ − 1)(3cos^{2}α − 1)] and the σ* intensity to the plane-orbital form I = A[1 − ¼(3cos^{2}θ − 1)(3cos^{2}γ − 1)] yields α = 74.2 ± 0.5° and γ = 67.9 ± 0.8° [Stöhr 1992]. Both angles describe the tilt of the ring normal from n̂, yet they differ by 6°. We attribute the difference to the window analysis itself, which integrates several overlapping transitions under one assumed orbital symmetry. The tensor model of Chapter 3 assigns each transition its own dipole and places the same tilt at γ = 69.6°, between the two window estimates. Window integration thus measures orientation, but its accuracy is set by the assumptions of the integration more than by the data, which motivates the tensor treatment developed in Chapters 2 and 3.',
notes=['Fig. 1.2a replaces the planned Fig. 2.4. The Chapter 2 sentence on initial and final states ("the initial state is commonly an atomic orbital deep within the atomic potential well") should cite Fig. 1.2a (T2.4).',
       'The 6° gap between α and γ is interpreted, not demonstrated, and the paragraph hedges it once ("We attribute").']))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 2
F.append(dict(
num='2.1', file='fig2-01_scattering-geometry',
where='Ch. 2, Sec. 2.1 (scattering in the far field). Replaces the opening paragraph of "Scattering fundamentals" that defines q.',
cap='<b>Scattering geometries and the momentum transfer.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> A plane wave with wavevector k scatters from a finite object, and the curved near-field wavefronts flatten into the far-field pattern recorded along k′ at scattering angle 2θ. The momentum transfer is q = k′ − k, with |q| = (4π/λ) sin θ. '
    '<b>b)</b> Specular reflection from a film on a substrate at grazing angle θ, for which q_{z} = (4π/λ) sin θ lies along the film normal. s polarization places E normal to the plane of incidence and p polarization places E in it.',
par='Every measurement in this work records intensity in the far field as a function of the momentum transfer q = k′ − k between the incident and scattered wavevectors (Fig. 2.1). For elastic scattering |k| = |k′| = 2π/λ, so the magnitude |q| = (4π/λ) sin θ is fixed by the scattering angle 2θ alone (Fig. 2.1a) [Attwood 1999]. In transmission RSoXS, q is read from the position of the diffuse intensity on an area detector, and the pattern reports in-plane structure through its dependence on |q| [Collins 2012; Gann 2016]. Specular reflectivity instead holds the detector at twice the grazing angle, so the reflected beam leaves at the angle of incidence and q = q_{z} lies along the film normal (Fig. 2.1b). To first order, scanning θ then samples the Fourier components of the depth profile of the optical constants, which is why reflectivity resolves stratification that transmission averages over [Wang 2005; Wang 2007]. The plane of incidence also defines the two polarizations used throughout. In s polarization E lies normal to the plane of incidence and therefore in the film plane at every θ. In p polarization E lies in the plane of incidence and carries a component cos θ along the film normal, largest at grazing incidence. For a uniaxial film the two polarizations therefore weight the in-plane and out-of-plane components of the optical tensor differently, which is the source of the orientation sensitivity used in Chapters 3 and 4 [Mezger 2011; Thelen 2020].',
notes=['Resolved. The draft definitions q = k − k′ and q = 2π sin(θ)/λ are removed in favor of this paragraph (replacement R1 at the end).']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.2', file='fig2-02_near-far-field',
where='Ch. 2, Sec. 2.1. Replaces "[figure with local E-field near field projections]" and the Fresnel-number paragraph after it. Per T2.1 the Born series, FEM, and surjectivity paragraphs are deleted, so this paragraph carries the far-field argument on its own.',
cap='<b>The far-field pattern emerges from local interactions between scatterers.</b> '
    'A plane wave with wavevector k and intensity I_{0} illuminates five scatterers of size a separated by distances of order d. The front scatterer scatters forward along k_{s}, so a deeper scatterer is driven by both I_{0} and this secondary field before it radiates along k′. '
    'Black lines are crests of the total scattered field from a two-dimensional multiple-scattering (Foldy-Lax) calculation, distorted near the cluster and flattening into outgoing wavefronts with distance. A detector at L ≫ d, a records only the far-field intensity I(2θ) (red), and the L axis is broken.',
par='The far-field condition is stated through the Fresnel number F = a^{2}/(Lλ), which compares the size a of a scattering feature with the distance L to the detector and the wavelength λ [Attwood 1999]. At 284 eV, λ ≈ 4.37 nm, and for a = 10 nm domains with L ≈ 0.5 m, F ≈ 5 × 10^{−8}. F approaches unity only for structures near (Lλ)^{1/2} ≈ 47 µm, far larger than any feature studied here, so every measurement in this work is a Fraunhofer measurement. This condition concerns the detector and says nothing about the field inside the sample. Figure 2.2 follows a plane wave through a small cluster of scatterers with a two-dimensional multiple-scattering calculation. Near the cluster each scatterer is driven by the incident wave and by the fields scattered from its neighbors, so the crests of the total field are distorted [Foldy 1945; Lax 1951], and a scatterer deep in the cluster radiates in response to both. With distance these distortions propagate out into smooth outgoing wavefronts, and the detector records only the far-field intensity I(2θ), a forward peak set by the spacing and size of the scatterers with weaker side maxima. The measurement therefore discards the phase of the far field and the structure of the near field, and the sample must be inferred by forward modeling and fitting, as described in the section on fitting. When the interaction between scatterers is weak, one scattering event per photon suffices and the first Born approximation A(q) = ∫ Δn(r) e^{iq·r} d^{3}r applies, which is the basis of transmission RSoXS and of its simulation [Gann 2016; Saurabh 2023]. When it is strong, as for specular reflection near the critical angle, the multiple scattering of Fig. 2.2 must be solved exactly, which the transfer matrix method of Fig. 2.11 does for a layered film.',
notes=['Numbers in the worked example are arithmetic from λ = 12398.4 eV Å / 284 eV = 43.66 Å (F = 4.6 × 10^{−8}, (Lλ)^{1/2} = 46.7 µm).']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.3', file='fig2-03_optical-constants',
where='Ch. 2, Sec. 2.3 (optical constants and tensors). Replaces "[Figure on the optical tensor and more]" and the sentence "This relationship is illustrated in the accompanying figure for a representative absorption edge". Place after the Kramers-Kronig paragraph.',
cap='<b>Optical constants of the Chapter 3 ZnPc film.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> In-plane β_{xx} (red) and out-of-plane β_{zz} (black) from the refined building-block model, with the bare-atom absorption (gray, dashed) and the pre-edge (270 to 282 eV) and post-edge (320 to 330 eV) normalization windows shaded. '
    '<b>b)</b> δ_{xx} and δ_{zz} from the Kramers-Kronig transform of β. The inset shows the bare-atom β from 10 eV to 30 keV that closes the transform, with the measured window in red. '
    '<b>c)</b> Relations between the refractive index n, the atomic scattering factors f_{1} and f_{2}, the permittivity ε, and the susceptibility χ, with β = λρμ_{m}/(4π) linking β to the mass absorption μ_{m}.',
par='Figure 2.3 carries these relations through for the ZnPc film of Chapter 3. The absorptive part follows from the mass absorption through β = λρμ_{m}/(4π) [Attwood 1999], with the measured spectrum scaled to the bare-atom absorption of ZnPc [Henke 1993] in a pre-edge window (270 to 282 eV) and a post-edge window (320 to 330 eV), where molecular bonding no longer alters the cross section (Fig. 2.3a). Between the windows the edge-on texture splits β into two tensor components. The π* resonances near 285 eV appear almost entirely in β_{xx}, since their transition dipoles lie along the ring normal and therefore close to the film plane, while the σ* band above 290 eV is stronger in β_{zz}. The dispersive part follows from the Kramers-Kronig relation δ(E) = (2/π) P∫_{0}^{∞} E′β(E′)/(E^{2} − E′^{2}) dE′ (Fig. 2.3b), evaluated by exact integration of a piecewise-linear β, a special case of the piecewise-polynomial method of Watts [Watts 2014]. Because δ at each energy depends on β at every energy, the measured window is embedded in the bare-atom β from 10 eV to 30 keV before the transform is evaluated (Fig. 2.3b, inset). The transform turns each absorption peak into a dispersive feature, so δ_{xx} reaches about −3 × 10^{−3} just below the first π* resonance and changes sign across it, and both components approach a positive, slowly varying value well above the edge. Contrast in a resonant measurement therefore changes in sign as well as magnitude with energy, and the energy of greatest contrast between two components need not coincide with an absorption peak. Figure 2.3c collects the equivalent descriptions of the same response, the atomic scattering factors f_{1} and f_{2}, the permittivity ε = n^{2}, and the susceptibility χ = ε − 1, so that results stated in any of these conventions can be compared directly [Attwood 1999; Yan 2013].',
notes=['Resolved. The Kramers-Kronig equation and the β equation in the draft are replaced (R2 and R3 at the end). The mass-absorption form is used throughout Chapters 2 and 5.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.7', file='fig2-07_alignment',
where='Ch. 2, Sample Alignment. Replaces "[insert figure demo of the challenges]". Place after the existing paragraph that ends "swap to this more time-consuming mode after the sample has been roughly aligned in the flat geometry," before the paragraph on realignment after polarization changes.',
cap='<b>Sample alignment with a finite beam and a finite photodiode, simulated by ray Monte Carlo.</b> '
    '<b>a, b)</b> Knife-edge geometry, in which the sample is raised through the beam at θ = 0, and the photodiode signal in 25 µm steps with an error-function fit, which places the surface at z_{0} = −0.7 ± 0.5 µm for a beam of σ ≈ 41 µm. '
    '<b>c, d)</b> Rocking geometry at fixed z_{0} with the detector at 0°, where light reflected from the surface (dark red) adds to the unblocked beam until a larger tilt sends it over the diode (gray, dashed), and the scan in 0.05° steps with a forward-model fit (red). The resulting cliff near θ_{s} = 0.29° shifts the half-maximum midpoint (gray, dashed) to 0.06° for a true offset of zero, while the fit (red, dashed) recovers an offset of 0.001 ± 0.003°. '
    '<b>e, f)</b> θ-2θ geometry with the detector at θ_{d} = 2°, where the whole beam reflects onto the diode near θ_{s} = 1°, and the scan in 0.04° steps with a soft-edged top-hat fit centered at θ_{0} = 1.003 ± 0.001°. Angles in the schematics are exaggerated.',
par='Figure 2.7 simulates the three alignment scans by ray Monte Carlo for a beam of σ = 40 µm with 1 mrad divergence, a 10 mm sample, and a 3 mm photodiode 150 mm downstream, to show where each scan places its center. In the knife-edge scan the sample is raised through the beam at θ = 0 (Fig. 2.7a), and the photodiode signal falls as an error function whose midpoint marks the surface and whose width returns the beam size (Fig. 2.7b). The fit places the surface at z_{0} = −0.7 ± 0.5 µm, well within one 25 µm step. The rocking scan at fixed z_{0} is harder to read (Fig. 2.7c). Tilting the sample unblocks the lower part of the beam and also reflects the part that strikes the surface onto the diode, so the signal keeps rising past the flat angle until the reflection passes over the top of the diode and the signal drops sharply (Fig. 2.7d). The cliff makes the peak asymmetric, and the half-maximum midpoint lands at 0.06° for a sample that is flat. At 285 eV this offset shifts every angle of a scan by the same amount, a 6 percent error in q_{z} at θ = 1°. The bias comes from the half-maximum estimator, which assumes a symmetric peak. A forward model of the same geometry, which integrates the beam profile and divergence over the sample and the diode aperture, fits the asymmetric curve directly with the amplitude, the angular offset, the beam size, and the diode acceptance free. It recovers an offset of 0.001 ± 0.003° and a beam of σ = 40.0 ± 0.3 µm (Fig. 2.7d, red). Fixing the diode acceptance at its true value reduces the uncertainty to 0.0013°, but an acceptance wrong by 10 percent then biases the offset by about 0.02° and more than doubles χ^{2}, so the acceptance is better left free. In the θ-2θ scan the detector is held at θ_{d} = 2° and the whole beam reflects onto the diode as θ_{s} passes through 1° (Fig. 2.7e). The signal is then a flat-topped peak with two edges set by the same geometry, and a soft-edged top-hat fit locates its center at θ_{0} = 1.003 ± 0.001° (Fig. 2.7f), about 33 times finer than the 0.04° step and without any model of the diode. Both fits reach sub-step precision because they use the known shape of the signal rather than the position of any single point, as in localization microscopy [Thompson 2002], and neither improves the resolution of two neighboring features, which remains set by the step. A model fit of the rocking scan therefore sets the angular zero in the flat geometry to a few thousandths of a degree, and the slower θ-2θ scan provides an independent check that does not depend on the diode geometry.',
notes=['Resolved. Panel d now carries a forward-model fit (src/rockfit.py, called from fig_v2_alignment.py). With the diode acceptance free the fit gives 0.0010 ± 0.0033° (χ^{2} = 32.8 for 21 degrees of freedom), with it fixed 0.0008 ± 0.0013° (32.8 for 22). Fixing the diode half height or distance 10 percent off gives offsets of ±0.020° and χ^{2} of 75 to 80. Values are in src/facts_align.json, and the previous artwork is in _to_delete/rev12_pre-rockfit.',
       'Resolved. The unrounded θ-2θ uncertainty is 0.00122°, so the step ratio is 33 and the earlier "roughly thirty" was right. The paragraph now says 33.',
       'The draft sentence on the half-maximum center is replaced (R4 at the end). The scans are simulated, and the caption title says so.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.9', file='fig2-09_reduction',
where='Ch. 2, Reflectivity reduction and stitching. Replaces the old Fig. 2.2 ("Reflectivity reduction schematic") and its caption. Place after the paragraph that ends "a set of overlapping measurements at the end of the prior and start of the current series," ahead of Stitching Details.',
cap='<b>Reduction of measured CCD frames into a stitched reflectivity curve, ZnPc at 285.0 eV in s polarization (Scan 85302).</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> A 2 × 2 binned frame at θ = 13.56° and 0.1 s on a logarithmic scale, with the specular region enlarged. Specular counts are summed in a 13 × 13 pixel box (solid), and the background is the mean of two equal boxes 28 pixels to either side (dashed). '
    '<b>b)</b> Background-subtracted count rate, counts divided by the exposure t plus the shutter offset t_{0} = 3.9 ms, for the four series and the direct beam I_{0} (star). Each series has its own exposure and higher-order suppressor position and repeats the last angles of the previous series. '
    '<b>c)</b> Stitched reflectivity with propagated uncertainties, mostly smaller than the markers, each series divided by I_{0} and by the running product of stitch factors S_{n,n−1}. Inset, R_{2}/R_{1} at the overlap angles and S_{2,1} = 48.7 ± 2.0 with its uncertainty (red band).',
par='Figure 2.9 follows one measured scan through this reduction, a ZnPc film at 285.0 eV in s polarization collected on the CCD detector. On each frame the specular beam is summed in a fixed box and the background is estimated from two boxes of equal size on either side of it (Fig. 2.9a). Dividing the background-subtracted counts by the effective exposure t + t_{0} converts each frame to a count rate. The shutter offset t_{0} = 3.9 ms is nearly four times the 1 ms exposure of the first two series, the limit of the original shutter, and cannot be neglected. The resulting count rates fall into four disjoint series (Fig. 2.9b). Within a series the rate follows the reflectivity, but at each series boundary the higher-order suppressor is withdrawn or the exposure lengthened, and the rate jumps by a factor that must be measured rather than taken from the nominal attenuation. The overlap angles provide that measurement. Each new series repeats the last few angles of the previous one, and the stitch factor S_{n,n−1} is the slope of an orthogonal-distance regression of R_{n} = S R_{n−1} through the origin on those angles (Fig. 2.9c, inset), as detailed below. Dividing the first series by the direct beam I_{0}, and each later series by I_{0} and the running product of stitch factors, yields a single reflectivity curve spanning more than three decades in the first 0.10 Å^{−1} (Fig. 2.9c). The stitch factors for this scan are S_{2,1} = 48.7 ± 2.0, S_{3,2} = 3.05 ± 0.04, and S_{4,3} = 1.13 ± 0.03. Their uncertainties enter every point of the later series as a common scale error, which a point-by-point uncertainty does not capture and which the error budget below identifies as a correlated term. The reduction shown here omits the dezinger and median filters and the beam-current normalization of the production code, pxr-reduce [ALS-RSOXS 2026], which implements the full routine described above.',
notes=['Resolved. I_{0} is taken at the shutter limit, 0.001 s for the original shutter used in Scan 85302 and 0.002 s for its replacement (R5 and R6 at the end).',
       'Resolved. The Stitching Details text now describes the ODR fit (section at the end), matching the figure and pxr-reduce. The pyref documentation describes an inverse-variance weighted mean of overlap points instead, so if Chapter 2 cites pyref for reduction, cite pxr-reduce for the stitch or align pyref with it.',
       'Frames, angles, exposures, and HOS positions are the measured ones from 17 November 2024. Only the first 0.10 Å^{−1} of the scan is shown.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.11', file='fig2-11_transfer-matrix',
where='Ch. 2, simulating reflectivity (3A.3). Replaces "[figure]" and "[Outline transfer matrix method]" under Calculating Reflectivity. Place after the paragraph that introduces the refloxide implementation (3A.1) and the isotropic Parratt case (3A.2).',
cap='<b>Multiple reflections make the exact solution of the layer stack necessary.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> Every interface of a stack with edge-on (layer 1) and face-on (layer 2) molecules reflects and transmits, and all reflected orders interfere at the detector. The field vector Ψ obeys ∂_{z}Ψ = i(ω/c)ΔΨ in each uniaxial layer, and interface matrices L_{i} and propagation matrices P_{i} compose to the stack matrix Γ_{N} that gives the reflection coefficients. '
    '<b>b to d)</b> s-polarized reflectivity at 285.1 eV from the transfer matrix (red) and the kinematic approximation (black, dashed) for the Chapter 3 single film (b), a 100 Å edge-on layer over a 90 Å face-on layer (c), and six periods of 40 Å edge-on and 40 Å face-on layers (d). The median deviation between the two is 0.23, 0.13, and 0.31 decades for b, c, and d.',
par='The kinematic picture fails for specular reflectivity because the reflected amplitude is not small. Below the critical angle θ_{c} = (2δ)^{1/2} most of the incident intensity is reflected, and at every buried interface part of the field is reflected back into the film, reflected again at the interface above, and returned toward the detector with a phase set by the round trip (Fig. 2.11a). The transfer matrix method accounts for every such order exactly. Within each layer the field vector Ψ = (E_{x}, H_{y}, E_{y}, −H_{x}) obeys ∂_{z}Ψ = i(ω/c)ΔΨ, where Δ is the 4 × 4 Berreman matrix built from the local dielectric tensor [Berreman 1972; Passler and Paarmann 2017], and its eigenmodes define the interface matrices L_{i} and the propagation matrices P_{i}. Their ordered product Γ_{N} = L_{1}P_{1}L_{2}P_{2}⋯L_{N}P_{N}L_{N+1} maps the field across the whole stack, and the reflectivities follow as R_{kl} = |r_{kl}(Γ_{N})|^{2}, valid because the incident medium is isotropic [Yeh 1980]. The isotropic limit reduces to the Parratt recursion [Parratt 1954], and the anisotropic form is the one used for resonant reflectivity of oriented organic films [Capelli 2016; Pasquali 2021] and implemented in refloxide [ALS-RSOXS 2026a]. Figure 2.11b to d compares this result with the kinematic approximation, which retains one reflection per interface, for three stacks at 285.1 eV. For the single ZnPc film of Chapter 3 (Fig. 2.11b) the kinematic curve exceeds unity below the critical angle and misplaces and deepens the first minima, although at higher q it reproduces the fringe spacing 2π/t ≈ 0.033 Å^{−1}. For the edge-on over face-on bilayer (Fig. 2.11c) the agreement is closer, with a median deviation of 0.13 decades, but the kinematic model distorts the low-q fringes and drives the minimum near 0.18 Å^{−1} below 10^{−8}. In the six-period multilayer (Fig. 2.11d) both models place the Bragg peaks of the 80 Å period near 0.079 and 0.236 Å^{−1}, but below the first peak the kinematic model predicts sharp oscillations that multiple reflections damp and redistribute, and the median deviation reaches 0.31 decades. The kinematic errors are largest near the critical angle and at the deep minima, the regions that carry much of a fit\'s sensitivity to density and interfacial structure (Fig. 2.13), so every simulation in this dissertation uses the transfer matrix.',
notes=['The 2π/t and Bragg positions are read from the figure and agree with t = 192.7 Å (2π/t = 0.0326 Å^{−1}) and a 40 + 40 Å period (2π/80 Å = 0.0785 Å^{−1}, third order 0.236 Å^{−1}).',
       'Berreman 1972, Yeh 1980, and Parratt 1954 are not in references.bib. Entries are in citations-to-add.bib.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.12', file='fig2-12_interfaces',
where='Ch. 2, interfaces and roughness (3A.4). Replaces "[Roughning models]", "[NC roughness]", "[DBW roughness]", and "[Graded roughness]". The existing roughness paragraph in Experimental Methods ("One feature of reflectivity arises from the nature of interfaces") is superseded by this one and should be cut or reduced to a forward pointer.',
cap='<b>Three interface models at the same roughness σ = 20 Å for a 192.7 Å ZnPc film on Si at 283.7 eV.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> Real-space interfaces drawn at equal lateral and vertical scale, with the wavelength λ = 43.7 Å and 2σ (red) marked. Névot-Croce describes fine roughness with a short in-plane correlation length ξ, Debye-Waller describes broad facets with a long ξ, and the graded interface describes interdiffusion without in-plane structure. '
    '<b>b)</b> Depth profiles of δ_{xx} as each model computes them. Névot-Croce and Debye-Waller compute the sharp profile (red), shown with the error-function profile usually drawn for them (black, dashed), while the graded interface slices the error-function profile into 1 Å layers and computes it directly (red), with the sharp profile for reference (gray, dashed).',
par='Real interfaces are neither flat nor sharp, and the three models in common use differ in what they assume about an interface and in where that assumption enters the calculation (Fig. 2.12). Névot-Croce and Debye-Waller roughness both keep the computed depth profile sharp and multiply each interface Fresnel coefficient by a damping factor, exp(−2k_{z,i}k_{z,i+1}σ^{2}) for Névot-Croce [Névot and Croce 1980] and exp(−2k_{z,i}^{2}σ^{2}) for Debye-Waller [Holý 1993; de Boer 1996]. The error-function profile usually drawn for these models (Fig. 2.12b, dashed) is a display convention that the calculation never uses, so the two models share one depth profile and differ only in the reflectivity they predict. The damping factors encode different interfaces. Névot-Croce assumes fine roughness with an in-plane correlation length ξ short compared with ξ_{diff} = λ/(1 − cos θ), and Debye-Waller assumes broad facets with ξ long compared with ξ_{diff} (Fig. 2.12a). At 283.7 eV, ξ_{diff} falls from about 10^{4} Å at θ = 5° to about 300 Å at θ = 30°, so the appropriate factor can change within a single scan and depends on lateral structure that specular reflectivity does not measure. The graded interface makes no claim about lateral structure. It slices the error-function profile into 1 Å layers and propagates the field through each one, so the depth profile is the computed one (Fig. 2.12b, right). This is the appropriate description of interdiffusion and of real composition or orientation gradients, and it serves as the reference for the multiplicative models. At σ = 20 Å the s-polarized reflectivity from the Névot-Croce and Debye-Waller factors departs from the graded result by up to 28 and 51 percent below q = 0.15 Å^{−1}, so at large roughness the factors are approximations to be tested against the graded model, consistent with numerical comparisons of these roughness treatments for X-ray and extreme-ultraviolet reflectance [Esashi 2021]. The refloxide selection guide states these regimes in terms of σk_{z} and ξ/ξ_{diff} [ALS-RSOXS 2026a]. In Chapter 3 the slab model applies Névot-Croce roughness at each interface, while the depth gradient is a physically motivated multi-slice profile of molecular tilt and density, computed slice by slice in the same way as the graded interface.',
notes=['The earlier note said ξ_{diff} is "several thousand Å at these angles." It is strongly angle dependent (λ = 43.7 Å gives 1.1 × 10^{4} Å at 5° and 330 Å at 30°), so the paragraph gives the range.',
       'The 28 and 51 percent figures come from the earlier figure note. If you keep the two-row figure, they are the only place the reflectivity comparison appears.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.13', file='fig2-13_objective-transforms',
where='Ch. 2, objective transforms (3B.2). Place after the inverse-problem paragraph that opens Fitting Analyses (3B.1).',
cap='<b>Transforming the reflectivity changes where an unweighted objective finds information.</b> '
    '<b>a to c)</b> Simulated s-polarized reflectivity of the Chapter 3 film at 285.1 eV (points, with noise σ = aR + bq) and the model (red), plotted as R (a), log_{10}R (b), and Rq^{4} (c), which removes the Fresnel q^{−4} decay. '
    '<b>d)</b> Misfit produced by a 2 Å thickness error in each representation, smoothed and scaled to its maximum, for R (black), log_{10}R (red), and Rq^{4} (red, dashed).',
par='An unweighted least-squares objective counts every point equally in whatever representation the data are fit, so the representation decides which part of the curve controls the fit. Figure 2.13 shows one simulated dataset, the Chapter 3 film at 285.1 eV with noise σ = aR + bq, as R, log_{10}R, and Rq^{4} (Fig. 2.13a to c), together with the misfit that a 2 Å thickness error produces in each representation (Fig. 2.13d). On a linear scale the curve is dominated by the points below q ≈ 0.04 Å^{−1}, and the misfit in R is confined to the critical-angle region, where thickness has little effect. On log_{10}R every decade carries comparable weight, and the misfit concentrates at the fringe minima, most strongly at the deep minimum near 0.135 Å^{−1}, where a small shift in fringe position changes log R by orders of magnitude. Multiplying by q^{4} removes the Fresnel decay, so fringe amplitudes are comparable across q and the misfit spreads over the mid and high q range. With correctly propagated uncertainties the choice matters little, since σ_{log R} = σ_{R}/(R ln 10) and to first order every representation yields the same weighted χ^{2}. It matters when uncertainties are absent, underestimated, or replaced by uniform weights, and because each transform also changes the implied distribution of the noise, the representation is part of the statistical model and is reported with it. Both transforms are available as objective transforms in refnx [Nelson and Prescott 2019], and both are used in this dissertation. The fits of Chapter 3 and the ZnPc comparison of Chapter 4 use log R, and the Alq3 fits of Chapter 4 use Rq^{4}.',
notes=['Resolved from lr-optical-models. scripts/alq3/run_alq3_reflectivity_fit_series.py uses Transform("YX4"), which is Rq^{4}, and scripts/znpc/run_znpc_fig4_fits.py uses Transform("logY").']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.14', file='fig2-14_optimizers',
where='Ch. 2, optimizers (3B.3). Place after Fig. 2.13 and before the posterior-estimation section, replacing "[Challenges with Naive MCMC sampling]".',
cap='<b>Four optimizers on the same sharp, correlated, and nonlinear reflectivity objective.</b> '
    'The surface is log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} of simulated s- and p-polarized reflectivity at 283.7 eV for the Chapter 3 film divided into a bulk layer of thickness t_{bulk} and a buried interfacial layer of thickness t_{int}, fit on log R, with a single deep minimum (star) at t_{bulk} = 178 Å and t_{int} = 15 Å. Every run starts from the same point (black circle, 195 Å, 30 Å). '
    '<b>a, b)</b> Levenberg-Marquardt (69 evaluations) and L-BFGS-B (48 evaluations) descend into the nearest valley and stop at the same local minimum, (210.4 Å, 14.1 Å) with log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} = 1.52. '
    '<b>c)</b> Differential evolution population at generations 0 (gray), 4 (light red), and 39 (red), which converges to the global minimum (178.0 Å, 15.0 Å) with log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} = −0.58 after 984 evaluations. '
    '<b>d)</b> Random-walk Metropolis chain of 4000 steps at the true likelihood, which reaches the minimum from this start but accepts 1 percent of its proposals in the sharp valley.',
par='Reflectivity objectives combine three difficulties, and Fig. 2.14 isolates them on a two-parameter problem. The Kiessig fringes make the objective sharp, since a few ångströms of thickness move every fringe. The total optical path of the film is well determined while its division between layers is not, which correlates the parameters into long, narrow, tilted valleys. Fringe aliasing repeats those valleys wherever the model fringes fall on the data fringes shifted by whole periods, each valley a local minimum. The surface in Fig. 2.14 is built from simulated s- and p-polarized data for the Chapter 3 film with its buried interfacial layer, and every optimizer starts from the same point, 17 Å and 15 Å from the true thicknesses. Levenberg-Marquardt, which interpolates between Gauss-Newton and gradient descent [Levenberg 1944; Marquardt 1963], and L-BFGS-B, which builds a limited-memory quasi-Newton Hessian within box bounds [Byrd 1995], both descend into the nearest valley and stop at (210.4 Å, 14.1 Å) with log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} = 1.52, after 69 and 48 evaluations (Fig. 2.14a, b). Differential evolution needs no starting point [Storn and Price 1997]. It evolves a population across the bounded space by mutation and crossover, and by generation 39 the population has collapsed onto the global minimum at (178.0 Å, 15.0 Å), log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} = −0.58, at a cost of 984 evaluations (Fig. 2.14c). A random-walk Metropolis chain [Metropolis 1953] also reaches the minimum from this start, but in the sharp valley it accepts only 1 percent of its proposals, so 4000 steps yield few independent samples (Fig. 2.14d). We expect a chain started in another valley to cross into this one rarely, which is the mixing problem of naive MCMC on reflectivity objectives [Machida 2024]. Local methods are therefore fast and precise only inside the correct basin, global methods find that basin at 14 to 20 times the cost, and sampling methods are best used to characterize a basin once it has been found. The Chapter 3 workflow follows this order, with differential evolution as the global stage and L-BFGS-B as the local polish.',
notes=['Surface parameters, for the methods text if wanted. Bulk γ = 69.6°, ρ = 1.64 g cm^{−3}, σ = 6.8 Å, interfacial γ = 38°, ρ = 0.87 g cm^{−3}, σ = 2 Å, on 15 Å SiO_{2} and Si, noise σ = aR + bq.',
       'The sentence on chains started in other valleys is an expectation, not shown in the figure, and is hedged accordingly.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.15', file='fig2-15_uncertainty',
where='Ch. 2, posterior estimation (3B.4). Replaces "[Using chained optimizers starting with differential evolution followed with L-BFGS-B, then MCMC for what it is good for]" and "[Other MCMC approaches such as dynasty]". Follows the Fig. 2.14 paragraph.',
cap='<b>From the global optimum to parameter uncertainty on the Fig. 2.14 objective.</b> '
    '<b>a)</b> Differential evolution with the population at generations 0 (gray), 4 (light red), and 8 (red), reaching (178.2 Å, 14.9 Å) after 270 evaluations. The red box marks the basin enlarged in b and c. '
    '<b>b)</b> The log_{10}χ^{2}_{ν} surface over that basin (0.83 Å by 0.46 Å) with the differential-evolution best point (star) and the local polish by L-BFGS-B (red, 27 evaluations) and Nelder-Mead (black, 58 evaluations), both converging to (178.00 Å, 14.99 Å). '
    '<b>c)</b> Affine-invariant ensemble MCMC in the same window, 32 walkers for 3000 steps after 1000 steps of burn-in, with an acceptance fraction of 0.72. '
    '<b>d)</b> Posterior samples with marginal distributions and 1σ and 2σ contours from the MCMC covariance (black, dashed), the finite-difference Hessian at the minimum (red), and the L-BFGS-B inverse Hessian (red, dotted).',
par='Figure 2.15 completes the workflow on the same objective by converting the global optimum into a parameter uncertainty. Differential evolution locates the basin (Fig. 2.15a), but its population carries no curvature information, so it returns a best point without an error estimate. A local optimizer started from that point refines it, and L-BFGS-B and the derivative-free Nelder-Mead method [Nelder and Mead 1965] converge to the same minimum at (178.00 Å, 14.99 Å) in 27 and 58 evaluations (Fig. 2.15b). Within this basin the objective is close to quadratic, the regime in which affine-invariant ensemble MCMC [Goodman and Weare 2010], as implemented in emcee and refnx [Foreman-Mackey 2013; Nelson and Prescott 2019], samples efficiently. Thirty-two walkers accept 72 percent of their proposals (Fig. 2.15c), against 1 percent for the random-walk chain on the full surface of Fig. 2.14d. The samples give σ(t_{bulk}) = 0.099 Å and σ(t_{int}) = 0.050 Å with a correlation of −0.63 (Fig. 2.15d), and the inverse of the finite-difference Hessian at the minimum reproduces these values to within 0.001 Å, as expected for a quadratic basin. The inverse Hessian reported by L-BFGS-B does not. It overstates σ(t_{bulk}) at 0.150 Å and understates the correlation at −0.40, because L-BFGS-B builds its Hessian estimate from the few steps it takes, and a run started near the minimum takes few steps. The internal estimate of a quasi-Newton optimizer is therefore a starting point to be checked against a sampled posterior or a Hessian computed at the minimum. Where the posterior has separated modes, as on the full surface of Fig. 2.14, nested sampling as implemented in dynesty [Speagle 2020] is the appropriate extension, since it explores separated modes and returns the evidence used for model comparison in the section that follows.',
notes=['Chapter 3 is unchanged. The sentence on quasi-Newton estimates is worded as a check rather than a rule.']))

F.append(dict(
num='2.16', file='fig2-16_automation',
where='Ch. 2, future work, merged with "Towards Agentic Reflectivity" (T3.9). Place after the paragraph on Gaussian-process measurement selection that ends "allowing their work to focus on uncovering material properties".',
cap='<b>Decision loop for autonomous reflectivity measurement.</b> '
    'A Gaussian process chooses the next condition (polarization, photon energy, and angle range) or the next sample, and a new sample is located before both branches pass to the alignment checker. After a polarization change the loop waits 15 min, and the sample is realigned whenever it fails the check at 1° and 2°. '
    'Each condition is measured one series at a time, with the incident intensity optimized for each series and data collected until the beam is lost, and a completed condition (red) returns control to the Gaussian process.',
par='Figure 2.16 assembles the procedures of this chapter into the decision loop planned for beamline 11.0.1.2 once its EPICS and Bluesky controls are in place [Allan 2019], following the cross-facility approach to autonomous scattering [Koepp 2026]. A Gaussian process holds the current state of knowledge [Noack 2019] and chooses either the next measurement condition, a combination of polarization, photon energy, and angular range, or the next sample. Both branches pass through an alignment checker that encodes the rules of the alignment section. A change of polarization changes the heat load on the optics, so the loop waits 15 min before checking, and the sample is realigned with the scans of Fig. 2.7 only when it fails a check of the reflected beam at 1° and 2°. A condition is then measured one series at a time. Before each series the incident intensity is raised through the higher-order suppressor and the exposure time, and the series runs until the reflected beam falls below the noise floor, which reproduces the series structure of Fig. 2.9 without a predefined list of angles. When a condition is complete, control returns to the Gaussian process, whose stopping rule asserts that every polarization and energy has been specified. Replacing that rule with Bayesian optimization on the fit itself would end the measurement when the depth profile, rather than the reflectivity, is determined.',
notes=['Branch labels on condition complete were corrected in the restyled artwork.',
       'Fig. 2.17 (beamline UI) is outside this batch and still needs a caption and a call-out.']))

