STITCH = dict(
where='Ch. 2, Stitching Details. Replaces "[Discussion of approaches and the math outlining the stitching process, how stitches are identified. How the scale factors are computed and more. Weighted Sums]". The second paragraph replaces the equation σ^{2}R_{n}(Q) = R_{n}(Q) and the line "expand this to actually break down error" under Accounting for Measurement Uncertainty.',
pars=['Each series after the first opens by repeating the last angles of the series before it, and these overlap angles fix the relative scale of the two series (Fig. 2.9c, inset). Repeated frames at the first angle of a new series are excluded, and the remaining overlap angles k give paired reflectivities R_{n−1,k} and R_{n,k}, both normalized by the same I_{0} but recorded at different higher-order suppressor positions or exposure times. The stitch factor S_{n,n−1} is the slope of the line R_{n,k} = S_{n,n−1}R_{n−1,k} through the origin, found by orthogonal distance regression [Boggs and Rogers 1990], which minimizes the summed squared perpendicular distances of the overlap points from the line. Both series carry comparable counting noise at the overlap, and an ordinary least-squares fit, which assigns all of the scatter to the ordinate, would bias the slope low through the noise in the abscissa. The standard error of S_{n,n−1} is taken from the covariance of the regression scaled by its residual variance, so it reflects the observed scatter of the overlap points rather than an assumed noise model. Series n is then placed on the scale of the first series by the running product of stitch factors, R_{n} = r_{n}/(I_{0} ∏_{m=2}^{n} S_{m,m−1}), where r_{n} = I_{n}/(t + t_{0}) is the background-subtracted count rate. The procedure follows pxr-reduce [ALS-RSOXS 2026], and the reduction of Fig. 2.9 performs the regression with the ODR module of SciPy [Virtanen 2020].',
      'The uncertainty of each stitched point combines independent and shared terms. For R_{n} as above, the relative variance is σ_{R}^{2}/R^{2} = σ_{r}^{2}/r^{2} + σ_{I0}^{2}/I_{0}^{2} + Σ_{m=2}^{n} σ_{S,m}^{2}/S_{m,m−1}^{2}, where σ_{r} combines the counting variance of the specular box with the variances of the row, column, and region background estimates, and σ_{I0} is the scatter of the repeated direct-beam frames. The first term changes from point to point. The last two are shared by every point of a series, so in a fit they act as a correlated scale error rather than as independent point uncertainties, although Fig. 2.9 adds them in quadrature to each point as the reduction convention. For the scan of Fig. 2.9 the stitch factors are S_{2,1} = 48.7 ± 2.0, S_{3,2} = 3.05 ± 0.04, and S_{4,3} = 1.13 ± 0.03, so the 4 percent uncertainty of the first stitch dominates the scale of every later series.'],
notes=['I could not open the pxr-reduce source (GitHub refuses automated reads of the source tree), so the description follows the reduction script for Fig. 2.9 (src/fig_v2_reduction.py), whose comments state that it reproduces pxr-reduce. Confirm the overlap exclusion and the unweighted ODR against pxr-reduce before submission.'])

